! ZM's normalized term and exponent bound, on the DOUBLE multiply.
!
! WHY A SECOND PROGRAM FOR THE SAME RULE.  p.99's ZM rule is
! implemented TWICE in StretchFP.bsv, in two different functions:
!
!     line 732   fpMul        (*  -- single-length multiply)
!     line 929   fpMulDouble  (D* -- double-length multiply)
!
! and the two lines are character-for-character identical:
!     r.zm = normalized && zero && (e > -1024);
!
! fp_zm_terms.strap covers the first.  NOTHING covered the second.
! Measured 2026-09-22 before this program existed: changing line 929's
! "(e > -1024)" to "(e >= -1024)" and running the WHOLE suite gave
! 3059 PASS, 0 FAIL -- the fault survived every test in the project.
! The mutation campaign could not see it either: the two ZM mutants in
! mutation_score.py use that identical string as their search pattern
! and apply() does replace(old, new, 1), so BOTH of them mutate line
! 732 and neither ever touches fpMulDouble.
!
! This is docs/NEXT.md's own trap -- "when a rule has more than one
! implementation, each copy needs its own witness", a list that already
! records ZM's clear at 16 copies -- caught on the copy the tooling
! was blind to rather than on the model.
!
! THE THREE CASES ARE fp_zm_terms.strap's, with D* for *.  The
! operands are unchanged: a zero product is zero whichever length the
! multiply keeps, and the exponent sum is the same, so the same
! constants exercise the same three branches of the same expression.
!
! p.99, via fp_zm_terms.strap's transcription of it: ZM is set by a
! MULTIPLY whose product is zero, only when NORMALIZED, and only when
! the exponent is GREATER THAN -1024.
!
! THE `*` TRAP APPLIES HERE TOO: a bare `D*` would be ambiguous the way
! a bare `*` is, so the modifier is always written -- D*(N) / D*(U).
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED: hand-written from p.99, not transcribed --
! the same standard as fp_zm_terms.strap, which it extends.

        SLC, (8)3300.0
!
! ---- case 1: normalized, zero product, exponent 0 -> ZM ON ---------
START   Z, $IND                      ! ZM is bit 33, so Z,$IND clears it
        L(U), ZF0                    ! accumulator := fraction 0, exponent 0
        D*(N), NZF0                  ! NORMALIZED double multiply, zero product
        BZB, $ZM, $                  ! hang up if ZM off -- the control
!
! ---- case 2: UNNORMALIZED -> ZM OFF -------------------------------
        Z, $IND
        L(U), ZF0
        D*(U), NZF0                  ! same operands, unnormalized
        BB, $ZM, $                   ! hang up if ZM on -- `normalized` is
                                     !   false, so the term must gate it
!
! ---- case 3: exponent exactly -1024 -> ZM OFF ---------------------
! THE CASE THAT CATCHES THE SURVIVING FAULT.  An injection changing
! line 929's "(e > -1024)" to ">=" passes cases 1 and 2 and fails only
! here.
        Z, $IND
        L(U), ZF512                  ! exponent -512
        D*(N), NZF512                ! -512 + -512 = -1024, the boundary
        BB, $ZM, $                   ! hang up if ZM on -- p.99 says
                                     !   GREATER than -1024, not >=
        B, PASS
!
ZF0     DD(BU,64), 0                 ! fraction 0, exponent 0
NZF0    DD(BU,64), 2251799813685248  ! fraction 2**47, exponent 0
ZF512   DD(BU,64), 4616189618054758400   ! fraction 0, exponent -512
NZF512  DD(BU,64), 4618441417868443648   ! fraction 2**47, exponent -512
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
